1.QUESTION 
Deadlock Condition

The deadlock occurs because:

*  P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
*  P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
   Both processes keep waiting for each other forever.

This is called Circular Wait, one of the four Coffman deadlock conditions.

* resource ordering
        lock(R1)
        lock(R2)
        Critical Section
        unlock(R2)
        unlock(R1)

2.QUESTION
* Debugging the Cause
   1. The processes are repeatedly moving between Ready and Waiting states.
   This indicates:

         a.  Processes are I/O-bound
         b.They frequently request I/O operations.
         c.CPU becomes idle while waiting for I/O completion.

     Hence CPU utilization decreases.

SOLUTION 
* System-Level Optimizations
     1. Increase Multiprogramming
         a. Load more CPU-bound processes.
         b.While one process waits for I/O,
         c.another process uses the CPU.

     2. Asynchronous I/O
         Use non-blocking I/O so the process continues executing while I/O is performed.

3.QUESTION
  * Root Cause
    The problem is not insufficient RAM.
    The issue is:
                  a . Large heap allocation
                  b.Many threads accessing different memory pages
                  c.Poor memory locality
                  d.Frequent page replacement

This causes excessive page faults.
  Optimizations (solution )
                1. Reduce Number of Threads
                Use:
                     a.Thread Pool
                     b.Worker Threads

                instead of creating many threads.

               2. Optimize Memory Allocation
                      a.Reduce heap usage
                      b.Reuse objects
                      c.Improve cache locality
                      d.Allocate contiguous memory

